## What happened

In **.NET 11**, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) now **throws at runtime on macOS**. DSA still works on Windows and Linux.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy DSA signatures, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Platform-guard DSA (keep legacy verification on Windows/Linux)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA verification not supported on macOS in .NET 11");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 2 — Migrate to ECDSA / RSA (recommended long-term)

Re-sign or re-issue with a modern algorithm and verify with:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

**Bottom line:** DSA is a legacy algorithm. If you must keep verifying old DSA signatures, gate the code with `OperatingSystem.IsMacOS()` and run it only on Windows/Linux. For new signatures, migrate to **ECDSA** or **RSA**.
